CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a compact three-dimensional vector diagram drawn on a set of coordinate axes. The z-axis rises vertically, the y-axis extends to the right, and the x-axis runs toward the lower left, out of the page; all three are thin black lines with small arrowheads and italic labels, meeting at an unlabeled origin. Two purple dots mark positions in space, P_1 sitting above and to the right of the origin and P_2 lower and farther to the right. A thick purple arrow from the origin to P_1 is labeled r(t_1) and a second purple arrow from the origin to P_2 is labeled r(t_2), both symbols carrying overhead vector arrows. A third purple arrow of the same weight runs directly from P_1 to P_2 and is labeled delta r. The three arrows close into a triangle, so the picture shows the displacement as exactly the vector that must be added to the earlier position vector to arrive at the later one. Casting the difference of two position vectors as a single arrow drawn between the two points extends the one-dimensional idea of displacement to motion anywhere in space.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - x-axis with arrowhead pointing toward lower-left (out of page), labeled 'x'
  - y-axis with arrowhead pointing right, labeled 'y'
  - z-axis with arrowhead pointing up, labeled 'z'
  - origin where the three axes meet (unlabeled)
  - point P1 — purple dot above and right of origin, labeled P₁
  - point P2 — purple dot lower and farther right, labeled P₂
  - position vector r(t₁): thick purple arrow from origin to P1 with label r⃗(t₁)
  - position vector r(t₂): thick purple arrow from origin to P2 with label r⃗(t₂)
  - displacement vector Δr: thick purple arrow from P1 to P2 with label Δr⃗
  - the three arrows closing into a triangle

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Axes span ±3 world units each: +Z straight up, +Y to the right, +X receding toward lower-left (out of page). Origin at (0,0,0). P1 at approximately (0.4, 1.8, 1.9) — above and right of origin; P2 at approximately (1.2, 2.9, 0.6) — lower and farther right. Both points are purple dots ~0.12 units radius. Vectors are thick purple (#7b2d8b, matching source magenta-purple) arrows ~0.06 units shaft radius with cone heads; labels r⃗(t₁), r⃗(t₂), Δr⃗ in italic purple beside each shaft midpoint, offset toward the triangle interior as in the source (r(t₁) label inside-left of upper edge, r(t₂) label inside-left of lower edge, Δr label right of the P1–P2 edge). Axis lines thin black with small arrowheads, labels x, y, z in italic black at tips.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "dragEndpoints",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "Moving P1 or P2 reshapes all three vectors at once, showing that Δr = r(t₂) − r(t₁) always closes the triangle no matter where the two positions lie in space"
  },
  {
    "id": "vectorSequence",
    "type": "button",
    "label": "Build displacement",
    "states": [
      "idle",
      "r1",
      "r2",
      "deltaR"
    ],
    "default": "idle",
    "teaches": "Revealing the vectors in order — origin-to-P1, then origin-to-P2, then P1-to-P2 — shows displacement as the change that carries the earlier position vector onto the later one"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbit",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating the scene confirms the points and vectors live in 3D space, not just in the plane of the page — displacement generalizes to arbitrary directions"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
